    "explanation": "Across the comparable ST1-ST5 store base, CONSUMER GOODS contributed 2,004,495.2523 of revenue growth, equal to 57.0647% of the total division-level revenue increase already established in the scan. The available drilldowns show that this gain was concentrated more by store than by any single visible product slice: ST5 alone contributed 1,640,544.3999 of the increase, with accompanying gains of 1,310,961.9075 in quantity and 417,212 in bills, while ST4 and ST3 added 331,057.9404 and 188,089.1037 respectively and ST1 and ST2 declined. Within the partial ranked product cuts, the largest positive contributors included product groups such as COOKING OILS (+256,169.5065), FRESH DAIRY (+189,487.0179), HEALTH AND BEAUTY CARE (+184,513.1886) and RICE (+159,017.6097), and special product groups led by COOKING OILS (+333,468.6813), SNACKS (+134,709.7851) and BASMATI RICE (+116,960.7951), indicating the growth is associated with a mix of grocery and personal-care lines rather than a single isolated section. Because the product-group and special-product-group evidence is partial top-N coverage, the strongest supported explanation is that CONSUMER GOODS growth was likely concentrated in ST5 and spread across several sizable merchandise groups, not driven by one product alone. Best follow-up check: run a complete, non-top-N section or product-group breakdown for CONSUMER GOODS within ST5 to identify the full set of sections behind that store’s outsized gain.",
